  views: {
    start_page: {
      heading_1: "Solar Monitoring System Website",
      text_1: "On this website you can store the data of a solar system and access them anytime and anywhere – without VPN or port forwarding on your own router. The principle is basically the same as with all other manufacturers: The data is transmitted from your home to the website, stored there, and graphically processed.",
      heading_2: "Basic information",
      heading_3: "Public solar systems",
      text_3: "Some public solar systems can be viewed here:",
      motivation_heading: "Motivation",
      motivation_1: "Myself, I built an off-grid system some time ago with devices that do not have an internet connection and wanted to be able to view the data at any time. But I am not a fan of opening ports on my home router or setting up a VPN just for this problem, so I decided to write a website.",
      motivation_2: "Also, setting up and understanding a VPN or port forwarding is not easily possible for laypeople. With this project I wanted to solve this problem as well.",
      registration_heading: "Registration",
      registration_1: "To give you the opportunity to register and set up your own system here, unfortunately you need an account on the site. The good news: I don't want any personal information, so only an e-mail address and a password are required.",
      acces_heading: "Permissions",
      acces_1: "After creating a system, you can decide whether it should be visible only to the user or to the public. I would of course appreciate public systems!",
      acces_2: "For public display, you can also choose whether only production is public and not consumption, because the latter could possibly indicate whether someone is at home.",
      open_source_heading: "Open Source",
      open_source_1: "The website's source code is of course open source and can be inspected. You can also run your own instance of the site if you have concerns about saving data here. These concerns are of course justified, but you can ask yourself whether the data from some solar system is really that exciting.",
      open_source_2: "The source code is available here on Github. Bugs or improvements can be submitted there.",
      clients_heading: "Clients",
      clients_1: "The data is transmitted to the server via REST. There are already various implementations and scripts available on Github.",
      clients_2: "Some of them are: Victron, EPever, SMA (YASDI), OpenDTU.",
      clients_3: "For OpenDTU, I have my own fork which enables data transmission.",
      detail_view: "to detail view"
    },
    registration: {
